  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA disturbance that has developed off of the southeast coast will\nbecome a strong low pressure system going into late tonight. A\nstrong easterly low level jet will develop ahead of this system,\nadvecting addition moisture from the Atlantic, resulting in\n1-1.5 PWATs. Steady rain showers can be expected, spreading\nsouth to north, starting Thursday morning and lasting into\nportions of Friday and Saturday. Current forecasted rainfall\namounts are around 0.75 to 1.25 inches in 36 hours from Thursday\nmorning through Friday evening.\n\nWhat will likely be more impactful will be increased gusty winds\nfrom this robust system. With the development of a strong\neasterly low level jet, we're seeing boundary layer winds\nforecasted to peak between 35 to 45 knots for higher elevations,\nwhich would transfer to surface winds of 20 to 30 mph, with wind\ngusts of up to 45 to 50 mph mainly for areas in NEPA. For\nCentral NY, forecasted winds will likely stay between 10 to 20\nmph, with wind gusts up to 30 to 35 mph possible. With\nincreasing confidence in the wind forecast, a Wind Advisory has\nbeen issued for Luzerne-Lackawanna-Southern Wayne-Wyoming-Pike\ncounties in Northeast PA, in effect starting 7am Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAlthough much weaker than the one expected on Thursday, another\nsystem is possible heading into the late weekend/early next\nweek. There's nothing spectacular about this system, mainly just\nthe next chance more widespread precipitation. With temperatures\nremaining warm, precipitation will likely remain as rain.\nSlightly above average temperatures are possible Sunday and\nMonday, with forecasted temperatures reaching into the low 50s.",
